WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.664
Scoring forecasts properly.

2
00:00:01.944 --> 00:00:05.187
Three practical rules for scoring the predictions you meet.

3
00:00:05.467 --> 00:00:07.429
Rewrite the claim until it can fail.

4
00:00:07.709 --> 00:00:10.163
“Transformative AI soon” cannot fail.

5
00:00:10.443 --> 00:00:21.835
“By 31 December 2027, an AI agent completes a task that takes a skilled human a month, with 80% reliability, on an independent benchmark” can.

6
00:00:22.115 --> 00:00:25.635
If you cannot rewrite it, the claim is a mood, not a forecast.

7
00:00:25.915 --> 00:00:28.027
Score with three outcomes, not two.

8
00:00:28.307 --> 00:00:34.323
Most bold forecasts come out “partly right”: correct in direction, wrong in timing or magnitude.

9
00:00:34.603 --> 00:00:36.181
That is useful information.

10
00:00:36.461 --> 00:00:41.987
It tells you to treat the next forecast from the same source as directional and to discount its dates.

11
00:00:42.267 --> 00:00:44.528
Judge calibration, not single calls.

12
00:00:44.808 --> 00:00:49.949
A forecaster who says 50% and is wrong is not discredited by one outcome.

13
00:00:50.229 --> 00:00:57.248
What you can evaluate is a track record: of the things a person said were 80% likely, did about 80% happen?

14
00:00:57.528 --> 00:01:03.437
Lab leaders rarely publish enough probabilistic forecasts to check this, which is itself worth knowing.

15
00:01:03.717 --> 00:01:05.403
And always attach incentives.

16
00:01:05.683 --> 00:01:12.552
Lab leaders benefit from short timelines when raising capital and from safety framing when shaping regulation.

17
00:01:12.832 --> 00:01:19.061
Critics (next lesson) benefit from contrarian visibility and sometimes from competing approaches.

18
00:01:19.341 --> 00:01:24.803
Incentives do not make a claim false; they tell you how much independent evidence to require.
